# quiet-bridge subscriptions — operator-provisioned routing capabilities.
#
# One line per destination:
#
#   <network-id> <terminal-hex> radio=<device-hex>[,…] internet=<device-hex>[,…]
#
# Every id is 32 bytes of hex and stays opaque to the daemon. What you are
# declaring is not who these people are — it is which side of the boundary
# each mailbox can be reached from, the one thing a blind gateway cannot
# work out for itself:
#
#   radio=     mailboxes whose owner is reachable only over the carrier.
#              The bridge FETCHES these (without draining them: the owner
#              may find internet later and must still get its mail) and
#              puts what it finds on the air.
#   internet=  mailboxes whose owner is reachable only over the relay.
#              The bridge PUTS into these what it took off the air, using
#              the same per-recipient hint any node would use.
#
# A destination with no internet= mailbox is refused rather than held:
# taking custody of frames that can never be delivered would be a promise
# the bridge cannot keep. Both lists accept several devices.
#
# network_id also names the segment's loop domain, so every gateway on one
# carrier must use the SAME value — that is how they recognise a shared
# forwarding domain and stop each other's echoes.

# beta-mesh-01 <terminal-hex> radio=<bob-device-hex> internet=<alice-device-hex>
